WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:03.135
-Our first guest tonight is an
Emmy-nominated actor and writer

00:00:03.135 --> 00:00:05.505
you know from his work on "The
X-Files" and "Californication."

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:09.141
His graphic novel "Kepler" hits
shelves December 27th,

00:00:09.141 --> 00:00:11.244
and he stars in "The Estate,"
which is in theaters now

00:00:11.244 --> 00:00:13.346
and available on demand
November 22nd.

00:00:13.346 --> 00:00:14.581
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:15.981
-Oh, God.

00:00:15.981 --> 00:00:17.350
He is coming to stay.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:19.118
-No.
-What's up?!

00:00:19.118 --> 00:00:21.554
-For [bleep] sake, not you too.

00:00:21.554 --> 00:00:23.823
-I came as soon as I got
Aunt Hilda's e-mail

00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:25.291
about her diagnosis.

00:00:25.291 --> 00:00:26.726
So sad.

00:00:26.726 --> 00:00:28.161
-You and Aunt Hilda
e-mail each other?

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:32.665
-Yeah, totes. We e-mail, text,
WhatsApp, all the apps.

00:00:32.665 --> 00:00:34.333
I wouldn't lose touch
with Aunt Hilda.

00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:36.002
I'm not a complete idiot.

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:41.174
Macy, mercy.

00:00:41.174 --> 00:00:46.546
You are looking merty, merty,
merty fine.

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:47.780
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:49.449
our friend David Duchovny,
everyone.

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:51.150
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:58.958 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:06.733 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:08.367
Welcome back.
-Thank you.

00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:10.637
You know, coming back to
this building reminds me

00:01:10.637 --> 00:01:13.072
I used to bartend at
Radio City Music Hall.

00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:15.041
-Did you really?
-Right after college, yeah.

00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:16.743
I did a lot of catering.
Did you ever do catering?

00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:18.812
-I waited tables.
I never catered, yeah.

00:01:18.812 --> 00:01:21.280
-So we would set up these --

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:22.881
just set up a bar
for intermission.

00:01:22.881 --> 00:01:24.416
That's all we could do,
just the intermission.

00:01:24.416 --> 00:01:26.619
But I saw all these great bands
come and play.

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:30.056
I saw, you know, Bette Midler,
who was amazing, and Peter Allen

00:01:30.056 --> 00:01:32.491
and Bryan Ferry,
and I saw Prince.

00:01:32.491 --> 00:01:34.860
And I would like to show you
how Prince did sound check.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.762
-Okay. Great.

00:01:36.762 --> 00:01:38.497
-This was like the 1999 tour.

00:01:38.497 --> 00:01:41.034
He was Prince, but he wasn't,
like, Prince.

00:01:41.034 --> 00:01:43.002
And not everybody knew him.

00:01:43.002 --> 00:01:44.370
And I watched him
do sound check,

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.205
and he had on
a long, like, overcoat.

00:01:46.205 --> 00:01:48.908
And he would do this --
The band would be playing,

00:01:48.908 --> 00:01:51.810
and he would, like, sidle
through the chairs to here.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.913
[ Laughter ]
And then he'd go like this.

00:01:56.515 --> 00:02:00.353
And then he'd go over here.
Then he'd go --

00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:04.023
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:05.959
That's Prince
doing a sound check.

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:09.762
[ Laughter ]
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:13.333
-That's actually -- If you ever
have the opportunity

00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:15.401
to audition for "SNL,"
where they will say,

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:16.869
"Do you have impressions
or characters?"

00:02:16.869 --> 00:02:18.704
I think a really funny
impression would be

00:02:18.704 --> 00:02:20.306
"This is Prince doing
a sound check."

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:22.575
-It's almost Andy Kaufman-esque.

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:24.243
-It's very Andy Kaufman-esque.
-Yes. Yeah.

00:02:24.243 --> 00:02:26.446
-It is wonderful
to have you here.

00:02:26.446 --> 00:02:28.781
This is a film about
a greedy family

00:02:28.781 --> 00:02:31.483
fighting over an inheritance.
-Good for Thanksgiving.

00:02:31.483 --> 00:02:34.620
-Very good Thanksgiving film.
You look very sleazy,

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.857
and the mustache is fantastic,
and I believe your own.

00:02:37.857 --> 00:02:40.392
-That is my own mustache.
-Yeah, very well done.

00:02:40.392 --> 00:02:42.195
You can tell a fake one,
and yours is very --

00:02:42.195 --> 00:02:43.696
-You can. You can.

00:02:43.696 --> 00:02:46.231
It was thicker than I imagined.
I didn't know I had it in me.

00:02:46.231 --> 00:02:47.333
-Did you really?
[ Laughter ]

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:49.502
So at no point
have you grown one?

00:02:49.502 --> 00:02:50.736
-Not like that.
-Yeah.

00:02:50.736 --> 00:02:52.204
-That baby was majestic.

00:02:52.204 --> 00:02:54.073
[ Laughter ]
-How long did it take?

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:55.408
How many hours are you putting
into that?

00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:57.076
-That's personal.
-Okay, gotcha.

00:02:57.076 --> 00:02:58.945
[ Laughter ]
Well, once an actor gives away

00:02:58.945 --> 00:03:00.713
a secret like that,
everybody has the mustache.

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:02.848
-Yeah, I don't have
many secrets.

00:03:02.848 --> 00:03:05.551
Yeah, I don't know.
I mean, I grew everything,

00:03:05.551 --> 00:03:08.054
and then we had a big powwow
in the makeup trailer

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:09.588
with the director
before going out there.

00:03:09.588 --> 00:03:11.557
And I was like,
"What's the funniest facial hair

00:03:11.557 --> 00:03:12.992
we can get to?"
-Yeah.

00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:14.594
-It wasn't about what's the best
facial hair?

00:03:14.594 --> 00:03:15.995
What's the funniest facial hair?

00:03:15.995 --> 00:03:19.899
We ended up on that fuller brush
kind of thing over my lip.

00:03:19.899 --> 00:03:23.669
But it was very similar to
another moment because there's

00:03:23.669 --> 00:03:26.138
a moment in the film where we
get this guy to expose his --

00:03:26.138 --> 00:03:27.473
his, uh --
-Yeah.

00:03:27.473 --> 00:03:28.775
-His penis.
-Yep.

00:03:28.775 --> 00:03:30.042
[ Laughter ]

00:03:30.042 --> 00:03:31.944
What was the word you were
looking for before?

00:03:31.944 --> 00:03:35.048
[ Laughter ]

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:37.884
[ Laughter ]

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:39.218
-I think I was looking for that.

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:41.353
[ Applause ]

00:03:41.353 --> 00:03:44.457
So the director was kind enough
to involve me

00:03:44.457 --> 00:03:47.326
in the conversations about what
is the best penis. Well, no.

00:03:47.326 --> 00:03:50.229
-The prosthetic?
-Yes. He had a tray of penises.

00:03:50.229 --> 00:03:51.497
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:52.765
-How many did they give you
to select from?

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:54.133
What are we talking? Five?

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:55.734
-I think it was four or five.
-Okay.

00:03:55.734 --> 00:03:58.871
-And the discussion wasn't --
Best penis is misleading.

00:03:58.871 --> 00:04:00.306
It was like
what's the funniest penis.

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:01.674
-Yeah.
-Which is a different

00:04:01.674 --> 00:04:03.576
conversation than
what's the best penis.

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:05.778
-Yeah.
-So we were kind of --

00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:07.613
-Yeah, I don't think there's
a single guy out there

00:04:07.613 --> 00:04:10.216
who is telling the ladies,
"It's so funny."

00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:12.217
[ Laughter ]

00:04:12.217 --> 00:04:14.486
-"You got to see it."
-"You gotta see --

00:04:14.486 --> 00:04:16.922
You are going to die laughing."

00:04:16.922 --> 00:04:18.724
-Oh, my God.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:18.724 --> 00:04:21.661
"I kind of want to show you
at dinner, but we can wait."

00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:24.364
-"We can wait. We can wait."

00:04:24.364 --> 00:04:27.566
-So, like, we're kind of like,
"Hmm." We got a tray there.

00:04:27.566 --> 00:04:31.270
We're like, you know,
giving it the 360.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:34.106
But I felt like, you know,
moments I'm sure you've had

00:04:34.106 --> 00:04:35.908
in your life where
you're like -- showbiz moments

00:04:35.908 --> 00:04:37.743
where like,
"I've kind of made it"?

00:04:37.743 --> 00:04:41.314
Well, when I was asked to judge
the funniest penis, I felt --

00:04:41.314 --> 00:04:42.881
[ Laughter ]

00:04:42.881 --> 00:04:45.451
Yeah, you know, this is
one of those moments.

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:47.820
-Right. I think because it's
only sort of like

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.123
above-the-title the title
actors or actresses

00:04:50.123 --> 00:04:51.356
who get to make
a choice like that.

00:04:51.356 --> 00:04:52.525
They don't let anybody --

00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:54.327
-Yeah, Meryl Streep is always
the one, like --

00:04:54.327 --> 00:04:56.462
[ Laughter ]

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:58.431
She's a famous judge of penises.

00:04:58.431 --> 00:05:01.200
-An incredible eye for it.
An incredible eye for it.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.202
-Sad penis too.
-Sad penis, yeah.

00:05:03.202 --> 00:05:05.704
Well, she's done a lot --
does a lot of dramas.

00:05:05.704 --> 00:05:07.940
So then you're like,
"Which is the saddest?"

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:09.241
[ Laughs ]

00:05:09.241 --> 00:05:10.910
-Sophie's penis.
-Sophie's penis.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:12.645
Oh, man.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:12.645 --> 00:05:14.147
-How many times can we say it?

00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:16.082
[ Laughter ]

00:05:16.082 --> 00:05:18.584
-No, no, that is Sophie's penis.

00:05:18.584 --> 00:05:19.685
There's two penises.

00:05:19.685 --> 00:05:21.120
[ Laughter ]

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.455
-And you have to give one up.

00:05:22.455 --> 00:05:26.725
[ Laughter ]

00:05:26.725 --> 00:05:29.294
-You know, it's weird.
In my head, I'm like, "How --"

00:05:29.294 --> 00:05:32.198
I don't remember how we got
here, but it did make sense.

00:05:32.198 --> 00:05:33.499
I have a lot more to ask you.

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:35.568
We'll be right back with more
from David Duchovny.

